From d364cff192535add63139d5b747a3a25fdfccaf5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tylnexttime <tylnexttime@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 07:49:21 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Marian P3 minimal: miss source with a pinned staging ring
 and a bounded pinned RAM tier

M1.0 measured upstream's cache at 84-91% VRAM hits but about 30% slower than
stock: each token spends 71-88 ms of host time uploading misses from a
page-faulting mmap (about 0.95 GB/s effective; copy probe: faulting mmap
0.41 GB/s, resident pageable 6.6 GB/s, pinned 10.3 GB/s). This patch changes
only where the bytes of a miss come from, behind an opt-in flag:

  --moe-miss-source mmap   upstream (default): upload from the host tensor
  --moe-miss-source ring   unbuffered reads into a pinned staging ring
  --moe-miss-source tier   the ring plus a bounded pinned RAM tier
  --moe-tier-mib N         tier size (pinned RAM)
  --moe-ring-mib N         ring size (default 64)

Reads (src/llama-moe-source.cpp): Windows FILE_FLAG_NO_BUFFERING with
overlapped I/O, 16 reads in flight; Linux O_DIRECT (pread). Offsets and
lengths are rounded out to the sector size queried from the volume
(FILE_STORAGE_INFO), buffers are sector-aligned, the expert is cut out of the
aligned read. A failed unbuffered open falls back to buffered reads with a
warning; a failed read uploads that expert tensor from the host tensor (the
same bytes) and is counted. The upload is the same ggml_backend_tensor_set_async
of nb[2] bytes to slot*nb[2] of the same bank as upstream, so the same bytes
reach the same slots and the same kernels read them.

Tier policy: the tier is split among the cache groups by host bytes, like the
VRAM budget, into fixed slots (one expert, all its tensors). Decayed counts
(halved every 256 decode steps of a layer) of the distinct experts selected in
decode steps (ubatch <= 8 tokens). A decode miss not in the tier is read into
a free tier slot, or into the slot of a CLOCK victim (reference bit set on
decode hits) only if its count is higher than the victim's (TinyLFU-style
admission); otherwise it goes through the ring. Prefill guard: steps above 8
tokens never count, admit or set reference bits; they only read the tier. The
tier is pinned, so a long prefill streaming the GGUF through the page cache
cannot evict it either. Slots read or uploaded from in the current step are
never victims; the ring is reused only after a synchronize.

Exactness checks: before anything else is pinned, the first and last expert
of every cached tensor are read through the source and compared with the
memory map; a mismatch drops the source (logged). MARIAN_MOE_SOURCE_VERIFY=all
compares every read during the run and aborts on a mismatch (never computes
with wrong bytes); =0 skips the load check. The source never throws (this
build has no /EHsc unwinding): on failure it frees its pinned memory and the
cache keeps upstream's uploads. The large-batch selective copy (prefill
ubatches above 32 tokens) still uploads from the host tensors: P3 minimal
covers the slot-map misses only.

Plumbing: llama_context_params gains moe_miss_source, moe_tier_size,
moe_ring_size; the model keeps the path of each memory-mapped file
(llama_model::weight_file_range), so a host tensor's file offset is its
address minus the map's base. The fill trace (MARIAN_MOE_TRACE_FILLS) records
the source of every fill: t tier, a admitted, r ring, m host tensor.

Tested CPU-only (no GPU): the source driven with the ggml CPU backend as the
device over the Swift 1.5 IQ2_XS shards, random decode and prefill-like steps,
3 configurations (tier 16/64/256 MiB, ring 1/8/64 MiB, verify=all once):
42,069 expert tensors through tier hits, admissions and ring wraps, every bank
slot equal to the memory map. Not yet run on the GPU.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
